@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Role of Garbage Collector in C#";
}

<!-- Section: Introduction to Garbage Collector -->
<section class="mb-5">
    <h2 class="text-secondary">What is the Role of Garbage Collector in C#?</h2>
    <p class="lead">
        The <b>Garbage Collector (GC)</b> in C# is responsible for automatic memory management. It reclaims memory occupied by objects that are no longer accessible, ensuring efficient use of system resources and preventing memory leaks.
    </p>
</section>

<!-- Section: Key Features of Garbage Collector -->
<section class="mb-5">
    <h2 class="text-secondary">Key Features of Garbage Collector</h2>
    <ul class="list-group">
        <li class="list-group-item">Automatically deallocates memory occupied by unreachable objects.</li>
        <li class="list-group-item">Prevents memory leaks and fragmentation.</li>
        <li class="list-group-item">Manages memory in three generations: <b>Gen 0</b>, <b>Gen 1</b>, and <b>Gen 2</b>.</li>
        <li class="list-group-item">Optimizes performance by running in the background.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<!-- Section: How Garbage Collection Works -->
<section class="mb-5">
    <h2 class="text-secondary">How Does Garbage Collection Work?</h2>
    <p class="lead">
        The Garbage Collector follows these steps to manage memory efficiently:
    </p>

    <ol class="list-group list-group-numbered">
        <li class="list-group-item">Identifies objects that are no longer reachable.</li>
        <li class="list-group-item">Releases memory occupied by these objects.</li>
        <li class="list-group-item">Compacts memory to optimize space allocation.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<!-- Section: Generations in Garbage Collection -->
<section class="mb-5">
    <h2 class="text-secondary">Generations in Garbage Collection</h2>
    <p class="lead">
        The .NET Garbage Collector organizes objects into three generations to optimize performance:
    </p>

    <table class="table table-bordered">
        <thead class="table-dark">
            <tr>
                <th>Generation</th>
                <th>Description</th>
                <th>Example</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Gen 0</b></td>
                <td>Stores short-lived objects. Frequent collections occur here.</td>
                <td>Local variables in a method.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Gen 1</b></td>
                <td>Serves as a buffer between short-lived and long-lived objects.</td>
                <td>Objects surviving Gen 0 but still needed.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Gen 2</b></td>
                <td>Contains long-lived objects that are rarely garbage collected.</td>
                <td>Static objects, database connections.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</section>

<!-- Section: Example of Garbage Collection -->
<section class="mb-5">
    <h2 class="text-secondary">Example: Forcing Garbage Collection</h2>
    <p class="lead">
        The following example demonstrates how to manually trigger garbage collection using <code>GC.Collect()</code>.
    </p>

    <!-- Example Code Block -->
    <h3 class="text-info">Example: Forcing Garbage Collection</h3>
    <div class="code-snippet position-relative bg-light p-3 rounded border">
        <pre class="mb-0">
<code>using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Creating objects...");
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
        {
            MyClass obj = new MyClass();
        }

        Console.WriteLine("Forcing garbage collection...");
        GC.Collect();
        GC.WaitForPendingFinalizers();
        Console.WriteLine("Garbage collection completed.");
    }
}

class MyClass
{
    ~MyClass()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Finalizer called, object collected.");
    }
}

// Output (Finalizer messages may appear later):
// Creating objects...
// Forcing garbage collection...
// Finalizer called, object collected.
// Garbage collection completed.</code>
        </pre>
        <button class="btn btn-outline-secondary copy-btn position-absolute top-0 end-0 m-2">Copy</button>
    </div>

    <p class="text-muted">
        The <code>GC.Collect()</code> method forces garbage collection, and the destructor (<code>~MyClass()</code>) confirms when an object is collected.
    </p>
</section>

<!-- Section: When to Use Garbage Collection Manually -->
<section class="mb-5">
    <h2 class="text-secondary">When Should You Use Garbage Collection Manually?</h2>
    <p class="lead">
        Although garbage collection is automatic, in certain cases, manually triggering it can be useful:
    </p>

    <ul class="list-group">
        <li class="list-group-item">When dealing with large temporary objects that consume a lot of memory.</li>
        <li class="list-group-item">Before entering a performance-critical section of code.</li>
        <li class="list-group-item">When ensuring memory cleanup before an application exits.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<!-- Section: Using IDisposable with Garbage Collector -->
<section class="mb-5">
    <h2 class="text-secondary">Using <code>IDisposable</code> to Manage Resources</h2>
    <p class="lead">
        The <code>IDisposable</code> interface allows explicit resource cleanup instead of relying on the garbage collector.
    </p>

    <!-- Example Code Block -->
    <h3 class="text-info">Example: Implementing IDisposable</h3>
    <div class="code-snippet position-relative bg-light p-3 rounded border">
        <pre class="mb-0">
<code>using System;

class ResourceHandler : IDisposable
{
    public ResourceHandler()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Resource acquired.");
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Resource released.");
    }
}

// Usage
class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        using (ResourceHandler handler = new ResourceHandler())
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Using resource...");
        } // Automatically calls Dispose() here

        Console.WriteLine("End of program.");
    }
}

// Output:
// Resource acquired.
// Using resource...
// Resource released.
// End of program.</code>
        </pre>
        <button class="btn btn-outline-secondary copy-btn position-absolute top-0 end-0 m-2">Copy</button>
    </div>

    <p class="text-muted">
        The <code>using</code> statement ensures <code>Dispose()</code> is called automatically, cleaning up resources.
    </p>
</section>

<!-- Section: Best Practices for Garbage Collection -->
<section class="mb-5">
    <h2 class="text-secondary">Best Practices for Garbage Collection</h2>
    <ul class="list-group">
        <li class="list-group-item">Allow the GC to manage memory automatically in most cases.</li>
        <li class="list-group-item">Use <code>IDisposable</code> for unmanaged resources like file streams and database connections.</li>
        <li class="list-group-item">Avoid excessive use of <code>GC.Collect()</code> as it can impact performance.</li>
        <li class="list-group-item">Use <code>using</code> blocks to ensure resource cleanup.</li>
        <li class="list-group-item">Monitor memory usage with profiling tools when optimizing performance.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

